  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the 26-year-old African-American woman, including a nonproductive cough, chest pain, hematuria, joint pain, and an erythematous facial rash, is suggestive of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). SLE is known to be associated with anti-histone antibodies, particularly in drug-induced cases, but more commonly presents with low serum complement levels (C3 and C4) due to complement consumption in active disease. The presence of hematuria and joint symptoms raises the possibility of lupus nephritis, which is also associated with low complement levels. Erosions of the metacarpophalangeal joints are not typical in early SLE, and cytotoxic glomerular antibodies are not characteristic of SLE. While sarcoidosis can cause hilar lymphadenopathy, the combination of symptoms is more indicative of SLE. Therefore, the most likely finding in this patient would be low serum levels of C3 and C4."
